  only [38;5;153m<script src="app.js" type="module">[39m.

  [1mOne breaking change, stated plainly:[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m gains a required third parameter. There's exactly one caller in this repo
  and it's updated in the same change, so nothing breaks — but if anything outside this repo calls [38;5;153mlogin()[39m, tell me
  now.

  [1mOne operational change:[0m ES modules don't load from [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so opening the app becomes [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m (or
  equivalent) rather than double-clicking [38;5;153mindex.html[39m.

  [1mSection 2 — Edges and tooling[0m

  [1mError handling.[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m throws in Safari private mode, when storage is disabled, and on quota errors. [38;5;153mgetUserId()
[39m  catches, falls back to an in-memory ID good for the page's lifetime, and warns once to the console. Login keeps
  working; persistence degrades silently rather than taking the form down with it. I think that's the right call for an
  ID that's for tracking — but say so if you'd rather it surface loudly.

  [1mTesting.[0m This is the gap: [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no dependencies, no scripts, no test runner, and no linter. There is
  nothing to hang a test on today. [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m has genuinely testable behavior — mints once, returns the same value on
  the second call, survives a reload, falls back when storage throws — and that's exactly the logic worth pinning down.
  Since the repo has no configured tooling, this is the cheapest moment to decide.
